---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Preface"
  description="Why this question took hold, in the first person. No claims, no evidence labels."
>
  <article class="page wrap">
    <header>
      <p class="label">Preface</p>
      <h1>序</h1>
    </header>

    <!-- No evidence labels on this page. Nothing here is a claim. -->
    <div class="body">
      <p>
        I did not choose this question. It arrived in a garden in Suzhou, on an
        afternoon when I was tired and not looking for anything, and it arrived
        as a physical fact rather than as an idea: I had been walking for
        twenty minutes and had covered perhaps eighty metres, and at some point
        my breathing had changed without my noticing.
      </p>

      <p>
        I have spent a long time since then trying to work out what did that.
        Not what it felt like — I know what it felt like — but what in the
        arrangement of walls and openings and paving was responsible, and
        whether it was in the arrangement at all or only in me, on that
        particular afternoon, being tired.
      </p>

      <p>
        My background is not in research. I studied advertising. I have spent
        years making things look like something — objects, exhibitions, cultural
        products for museums and gardens — which is a training in surfaces, and
        I am aware that it has left me able to produce the appearance of depth
        rather quickly. It is the habit I most distrust in myself, and it is
        also, I think, why I notice framing and sequencing at all. You learn to
        see the devices when you have spent years operating them.
      </p>

      <p>
        A few years ago I started building instead of designing, which mostly
        meant learning to program badly and in public. The things on this site
        are the result. They are small, they run in a browser, and each one
        exists because I could not think my way to an answer and wanted to find
        out what the thing would do if it existed. Most of what I have learned
        came from the versions that did not work.
      </p>

      <p>
        What I do not know is substantial and I would rather say it here than
        have it inferred. I do not know whether any of this affects anyone. I
        have never measured a person. I do not know whether the principles I
        take from gardens and rituals survive being moved onto a screen, or
        whether I am collecting motifs and calling them mechanisms. I do not
        know whether the fact that a thousand years of practice arrived at these
        forms is evidence that they work, or only evidence that they were
        available.
      </p>

      <p>
        There is also a question underneath this one that I am not equipped to
        approach directly and cannot stop approaching indirectly, which is why
        people suffer, and what has ever helped. I mention it because it is the
        actual horizon, and because a research practice that pretends its
        horizon is nearer than it is will keep producing answers to questions
        nobody asked.
      </p>

      <p>
        I am giving this ten years. Not because ten years is the right number
        but because it is long enough that I cannot get to the end of it by
        being clever, and I have noticed that being clever is my usual way out.
      </p>

      <p class="sign">
        TAO<br />
        <span class="mono">2026</span>
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
